tim looked for the cartoon all night , but he could not find it . he was very tired and cold . in the end , tim did not find his cartoon and had to go to bed without watching it . tim had a bad night , and he wished he could find his favorite cartoon again . once upon a time , there was a big hippo named hippy . hippy loved to dance and play with his friends . one day , hippy was dancing in the forest , and all his friends were watching .

while hippy was dancing , he saw a bird lying on the ground . the bird was not moving . hippy 's friend , bunny , told him that the bird was dead . hippy felt sad for the bird and stopped dancing . he wanted to help the bird , but he did n't know how .

hippy and his friends decided to make a nice place for the bird to rest . they put flowers and leaves around the bird . then , they all stood around the bird and clapped their hands . they were applauding the bird for all the joy it brought to the forest . the moral of the story is to always remember to be kind and show love to others , even when they are gone . once upon a time , there was a lucky cloth . the lucky cloth was very pretty and soft . it lived in a big house with a little girl named sue . sue loved her lucky cloth very much . she took it with her everywhere she went . it made her feel happy and safe .

one day , sue and her lucky cloth went to the park . they played on the swings and the slide . sue was having so much fun . then , sue saw a little boy named tim sitting on a bench . he was sad and crying . sue went up to tim and asked , " why are you sad ? "

tim looked at sue and said , " i lost my toy . i ca n't find it anywhere . " sue wanted to help tim feel better . she showed him her lucky cloth and said , " this is my lucky cloth . it helps me when i am sad . maybe it can help you find your toy . "

tim touched the lucky cloth and smiled . together , sue and tim looked for the toy . they searched the park until they found it under a big tree . tim was so happy to have his toy back . he hugged sue and said , " thank you for helping me . your lucky cloth is very special . "

from that day on , sue and tim became best friends . they always played together and shared their toys . and , of course , sue 's lucky cloth was always with them . the lucky cloth made them feel happy and safe , and they knew they could always count on each other . once upon a time , there was a little girl named lily . she had a toy model of a big , heavy elephant . lily loved her toy elephant very much .

one day , lily and her friend tim were playing with the toy elephant . tim asked , " how much does the elephant weigh ? " lily thought for a moment and said , " i do n't know , but it is very heavy ! "

so , lily and tim decided to find out how much the elephant weighed . they put the toy elephant on a scale and saw that it weighed five pounds . they were very happy to know how heavy the toy was .

as they played , lily and tim learned that it is good to share and help each other . they both had fun and became even better friends . the moral of the story is that sharing and helping others brings happiness to everyone . once upon a time , in a small town , there was a peaceful school . the school had many children who played , laughed , and learned together . they liked to sing songs , draw pictures , and listen to stories .

one day , a little bug came to the school . the bug wanted to learn and make friends too . he would crawl on the floor , under chairs , and even up the walls . the children saw the bug and giggled as they watched him crawl around the room .

the teacher saw the bug and knew he wanted to be part of the peaceful school . she let the bug stay , and the children loved their new friend . they learned about bugs , and the bug learned about the children . and so , the peaceful school became even more special with their little crawling friend . once upon a time , there was a loyal dog named buddy . buddy liked to swim in the big pond . every day , buddy would run and jump into the water . he loved the way the water felt on his fur .

